The message queue keeps its place at the readable size, and its foot is the
same margin as its side (ROCKNIX fork #295, #296; the eighth cut).

The box follows the font, as upstream draws it: the sixth cut scaled the box
down with the placement and put 14 px of text in 31 px of box. The room under
the bottom box is the box's distance from the panel's left edge
(msg_queue_rect_start_x: 12 px on a 640x480 panel with the 13 px face), so the
two read as one margin -- upstream's padding*4 put the foot 42 px up before
the floor and 57 under it, and the maintainer chose the left margin's number
from mock-ups. Every box above the bottom one sits one divider up: the pitch
upstream's quarter-height spacing gives when the height divides by four,
without the rounding that ceilf'd the sixth cut's seam away (a 62 px height
made a 31.5 pitch under a 31 px box) and without the first cut's placement
spacing under the floor's box (a 36 pitch under 40: the boxes overlapped by
four rows). With the margin tied to the drawn face, the placement reference
of the earlier cuts is gone: RetroArch's two layout passes at a launch now
differ here by one pixel (the first pass's regular face is smaller), where
the sixth cut's 9 px reference made them differ by 14 and a message pulled
between the passes jumped when the next arrived; and each layout now places
the messages already on screen again, so none keeps the first pass's place.
Measured per build at the H700's widget scale in docs/qa-frames/2026-09-27
on the fork. The placement's log line gives the numbers the stack is placed
by -- both box heights and their pitch, the divider included -- once the
divider is known; the face's size is 0017's line.

